WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.868
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.063 --> 00:00:09.230
SPEAKER 1: If you are looking
to run queries on data

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:11.950
not already part of public
or shared data sets,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.710
you must first load
your data into BigQuery.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:17.380
BigQuery accepts data
in several formats.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.990
This video will cover how
to load data from a CSV

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:23.870
file on your local machine into
BigQuery, using the web UI.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:25.960
This process requires
a Google Cloud Platform

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.875
project with billing enabled.

00:00:27.875 --> 00:00:30.250
Within your project, you may
take advantage of the Google

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.680
Cloud free tier and
bypass cost by staying

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.920
within the tier limits for
Cloud Storage and BigQuery.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:37.750
Loading data into
BigQuery via the web

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:41.110
UI can be done two ways, first
by loading the data directly

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:43.900
from your local machine, or
second by using Google Cloud

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.940
Storage as an intermediary.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.490
Direct uploads from your
computer using the BigQuery web

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:51.010
UI are limited to 10 megabytes.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.870
For this reason,
this video will focus

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:56.080
on loading data into BigQuery
by way of Google Cloud Storage,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.910
which doesn't have this limit.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.610
Get started in the
BigQuery web UI.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.440
Identify the data set
to which you would

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.260
like to add the new table.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.890
If using an existing data
set, note the data location.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:12.010
The data location will be
needed in a future step.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.530
If you need to create a
new data set, do so now.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.930
To create a new data set,
select the project name

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.720
in the left-hand nav, and
then click the Create Data Set

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:21.830
button.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:27.180
Name the data set, choose
the data set location,

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.620
and then click Create Data Set.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.196
The new data set will shortly
appear in the left-hand nav.

00:01:34.196 --> 00:01:37.250
Next, navigate to cloud
storage, by typing "storage"

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.820
into the search
bar in the console.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.180
When you arrive, click
the Create Bucket button.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:53.880
Name your bucket, then
choose the location.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.240
You must choose a location
for your cloud storage bucket

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.930
in the same region as the
location of the BigQuery data

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.260
set.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.750
The bucket can be either
a multi-regional bucket,

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:06.290
or a regional bucket in the
same region as the data set.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:09.330
One exception, you can load
data in a US-based data

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.180
set from any region.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.580
Since you are creating this
bucket for the sole reason

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.650
to transfer the
data into BigQuery,

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:17.450
save on egress
charges by choosing

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.760
the exact same location
as your destination

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.130
data set in BigQuery.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:25.970
In this demo, that
is Multi-region US.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:30.500
Choose the Standard Storage
Class, then click Create.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.870
Now, you may click the
Upload Files button

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.670
to select the file
you want to upload,

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.852
or simply drag the
file from your desktop

00:02:36.852 --> 00:02:38.435
onto the Google Cloud
Storage browser.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.950
Once the upload
finishes, navigate back

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:45.180
to the BigQuery web UI.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.615
Select your data set,
and click Create Table.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:58.620
Choose Google Cloud
Storage as your source.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:06.820
Click browse, double-click
on the storage bucket,

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:08.020
and select your data object.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.480
The file format should
update automatically.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:15.740
Provide a table name.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.170
You can either provide
the schema details,

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.020
or click the box to
Auto Detect the Schema.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.390
At this time, you may choose
to partition and cluster

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:27.320
your data.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.510
A video outlining
the hows and whys

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.400
of partitioning and
clustering is linked

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.690
in the video description below.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.510
If your CSV file
has a header row,

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:38.570
display the Advanced Options and
add the number of head arrows

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.540
in the Header Rows
to Skip field.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.970
There are also several other
options you can choose,

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:47.820
including Alternative Field
Delimiters Based on your File.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.890
Now click Create Table.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:51.185
A load job will be created.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:59.730
When the job finishes, you can
view the table schema, details,

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.660
and a preview of the data.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:03.630
Now your table is
ready to be queried.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.150
Click Query Table, and a
preloaded query statement

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.100
will appear in the
Query Editor, which

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.260
you can begin editing directly.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.270
Before diving into
the data, consider

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.880
if you would like to delete
the file you originally loaded

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.920
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.899
Depending on your
situation, it can also

00:04:18.899 --> 00:04:21.120
be useful to keep
it as a backup.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.420
Both Cloud Storage and BigQuery
storage have separate pricing,

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.690
so be sure to review the pricing
documentation linked below.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:30.600
Thanks for watching,
and happy analyzing.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

